 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050490 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050490 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050490 #edb/ref 1031 "Limits of Logarithmic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050490 #edb/ref 1033 1377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050490 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4d8d1b92-7074-5d18-8e35-15cd34d42567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113010 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113010 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113010 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e8ec9261-2a03-5162-a251-fb90e85d9515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113011 #edb/ref 1080 "images/d7/d7336172bda3512c455830f0b1be46deac8d0522294d270f3f734391a60bc853.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113011 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113011 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a9633bb3-7ee4-57c3-a6fd-cc54be9e9bbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113012 #edb/ref 1080 "images/f7/f7c2d23d12059c5fe56ce44757825e6c95517e842caeded02b734bbfdc5f7945.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113012 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113012 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ab903738-aa5f-582e-a489-750e0b0218bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113013 #edb/ref 1080 "images/8b/8b9cffb10d9396ed518f109a906dd4c04187c4359cd9e7b1396153bc4b38a1d3.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113013 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113013 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b3ae7a21-5fda-5426-8a2c-e1dd1e1f05de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113014 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2c/2c59cd038a9eddad9c7834eb33a4ceb380fa764127fe71edf7a866f153160abb.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113014 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113014 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b97d167f-b5dd-5760-af3b-6ec9251b03fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113015 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ce/ceceb8e7d30739ea2a23a3adf4ffc2d9896b32cf1846af7865fcd473e9b8e3e2.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113015 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113015 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5450386b-555a-55d4-8e20-44e905317424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115105 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115105 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the curve of $y=\\log_7 (x+4)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4228cae9-1914-59e9-8ed6-48b3edf92638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115105 #edb/ref 1074 "To plot\n\n$$\ny=\\log_7 {(x+4)}\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\log_7 {x}$.\n\n![](images/97/975f58b320e2293dbfaf267a3acfe9187438ae40add3603bac98d142b35c44c1.png)\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\log_7 {(x+4)}$.\n\n![](images/ce/ce5db8eb1adb7e84337427c1c769eabec9cb420b51a7e867f47e8d573c697a79.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115106 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115106 #edb/ref 1071 "The diagram below shows the graph of $y=k \\log_5 (bx)$. Find the value of $b\\cdot k$.\n\n![](images/36/3698fc49edb443517ed0ee2cfa81c586c428f402df7028a8ec4b47702d8a4fad.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d064137-5763-57d8-9b8e-ea145c323427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115106 #edb/ref 1074 "Clearly,\n\n$$\nb \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac1b\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= k\\log_{5} (b \\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\\n&= k\\log_{5} 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nlies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence,\n\n$$\n(2,0) = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk\\log_{5} (bx) &= y \\\\\nk\\log_{5} (\\frac{1}{2}x) &= y \\\\\nk\\log_{5} (\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10) &= 3 \\\\\nk\\log_{5} 5 &= 3 \\\\\nk &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115147 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115147 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4\\log_3 (x-2)+1$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd143c19-b7b4-5e50-9e22-10f8c505c047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115147 #edb/ref 1074 "To plot\n\n$$\ny=4\\log_3 (x-2)+1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\log_3 x$.![](images/45/452610c472df847bb7a09815444c3d4f318008a8881864f668f153bee6cbae33.png)\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=\\log_3 (x-2)$.![](images/5b/5bf8eba8adaa2e7221d77eca13bef8ee33a37ed05af2071e12fec12f4edb672d.png)\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4\\log_3 (x-2)$.![](images/90/90bd455c3a6b833b169c3207c58cc422c3fb963c7de9ae5f11bc0b6464117d6f.png)\n- Finally, translate the result by $1$ unit up to get the graph of $y=4\\log_3 (x-2)+1$.![](images/ad/ade4b0bff8907d7284cedd15895e997013121f0f91f2b82a0314f363a81ad3a9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115148 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115148 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bd/bd74c5b1a7233be5978a128a74956fa953cd78a737b8f40f68c1e42993f58140.png)\n\nGiven the diagram above, what is the value of $k$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115148 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "031f2ffe-6dd5-5b4f-93c0-3c1a6f1edf23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115148 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of\n\n$$\ny=k\\log_2 {x}\n$$\n\ncan be obtained from\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2 {x}\n$$\n\nby a vertical stretch of scale factor $k$.\n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(4,2)$ that lies on\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2 {x}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\n(4,k \\cdot 2)=\\left(4,3\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on\n\n$$\ny=k\\log_2 {x}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac 32\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115282 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115282 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115282 #edb/ref 1039 "Graphing a Translated or Stretched Logarithmic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115282 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115282 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f9e91fd-9896-5fdd-8a60-b8ec86ca761c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115283 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Transformed Logarithmic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115283 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115283 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f44d9ddf-a830-559a-807e-a5a3eaaf66e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115324 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115324 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115324 #edb/ref 1039 "Combining Translations and Stretches of Logarithmic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115324 #edb/re